# DNS observability follow-up

The UDP DNS parser correlates requests with a canonical key made from
the network-namespace cookie, address family, client/resolver addresses
and ports, and transaction ID, and attributes the answer to the querying
socket's owner. The items below are the remaining parser and
correlation gaps. `testing/upatch/kernel-obs/dns/dns_obs_matrix.py` now validates
case construction and batch encoding only. Its former target runner used
`BPF_PROG_TEST_RUN`, which is restricted to Shirudo. The target suite uses
live loopback UDP exchanges for representative query and response
coverage. Exact malformed-packet and mutation-fuzz coverage needs a live
packet harness before it can gate target changes.

## Matching and response integrity

- [ ] Decode and compare the response question name with the stored request.
      DNS comparison must be ASCII case-insensitive and must safely handle
      compression, pointer loops, and invalid pointer targets.
- [ ] Require query class IN and compare response qclass with the request.
- [ ] Validate response opcode, RCODE, and TC before accepting answers. Define
      whether truncated responses produce no event or an explicitly partial
      event.
- [ ] Constrain emitted addresses to the requested family: A queries may emit
      A records and AAAA queries may emit AAAA records, while still skipping
      CNAME and other intermediate records.
- [ ] Add request age to the pending value and reject stale responses. This
      prevents a delayed response from matching a later reuse of the exact
      same flow and transaction ID.
- [ ] Decide whether exact-flow, same-ID concurrent requests need multi-value
      storage. Standards-compliant clients should avoid this reuse, but the
      current map still replaces the older request.

## Packet validation

- [ ] Reject IPv4 and IPv6 fragments unless complete reassembly is guaranteed
      before the cgroup hook.
- [ ] Enforce IPv4 total length, IPv6 payload length, and UDP length against
      `data_end`; reject inconsistent or truncated packets.
- [ ] Enforce the intended 1232-byte UDP parsing budget on the actual packet,
      not only on individual logical offsets.
- [ ] Walk supported IPv6 extension headers or reject packets containing any
      extension header with a dedicated debug counter.
- [ ] Validate the IP version against the Ethernet protocol.
- [ ] Decide whether checksum validation belongs in this program. The case
      builder includes invalid-checksum inputs, but target tests do not currently
      inject those malformed packets over a live network path.

## Name and answer parsing

- [ ] Reject embedded NUL bytes in labels so stored names cannot be truncated
      by C-string serialization.
- [ ] Validate every compression pointer target even when the owner name is
      only skipped, including bounds, loops, and maximum traversal depth.
- [ ] Make the 75-answer cap observable with a counter or explicit truncation
      status instead of silently ignoring later answers.
- [ ] Define atomic response behavior. At present, valid early answers remain
      recorded when a later answer is malformed.

## Capacity and observability

- [x] Table overflow and lost records are reported in every export header.
- [ ] Add counters for request-map update failure, LRU displacement, stale
      response rejection and tuple mismatch.
- [ ] Stress the 1024-entry pending-request LRU and document expected loss
      behavior under sustained concurrent DNS traffic.
- [ ] Add namespace-isolation coverage proving identical tuples and IDs in two
      network namespaces remain independent.

## Out of scope unless requirements change

- DNS over TCP, TLS, or HTTPS; the public DoH resolvers are on the
  destination watch list instead.
- DNS traffic on ports other than 53, including mDNS and LLMNR.
- Full recursive DNS interpretation beyond correlating A/AAAA responses.
